@startuml
!theme vibrant

title Design Evolution: From "Clunky" to "Cached"

start

:Initial Idea: **`infer_and_register_type(value)`**;
note right #PALEVIOLETRED
  The user feels this is "clunky".
end note

partition "Responsibilities (All in One Function)" #PALEVIOLETRED {
  :Finds rule for value;
  :Registers adapter with `sqlite3` (global side effect);
  :Caches the result in `self.type_map`;
  :Returns the SQL type string;
}

-> **Critique:** Function has too many responsibilities.
Lacks separation of concerns.;

partition "Refactor #1: Separation of Concerns" #PALETURQUOISE {
  split
    partition "New Method 1" #LightSkyBlue {
      :**`_ensure_type_is_registered(value)`**;
      note left #LightSkyBlue
        Handles the **action**:
        - Loops through rules.
        - Calls `sqlite3.register_adapter`.
        - Updates `_registered_types` set.
      end note
    }
    split again
    partition "New Method 2" #LightSkyBlue {
      :**`_infer_type(value)`**;
      note right #LightSkyBlue
        Handles the **query**:
        - Loops through rules.
        - Returns SQL type string.
        - Updates `type_map` cache.
      end note
    }
  endsplit
}

-> **User Insight:** The two methods loop through the same rules.
This creates a **"double loop"** on the first encounter with a new type.;

partition "Next Solution: The 'Learn Once' Pattern" #PALEGREEN {
  :New Method: **`_learn_type(value)`**;
  partition "Optimized Logic (Single Loop)" #PALEGREEN {
    :Check a central cache (`_rule_cache`) for the type;
    if (Type cached?) then (yes)
      :**(Fast Path)**
      Return cached SQL type;
    else (no)
      :**(First Encounter)**
      Loop through rules **ONCE**;
      :When a rule matches:
      1. Register the adapter.
      2. Cache the **entire result** (type & adapter).
      3. Return the SQL type string;
    endif
  }
}

-> **Conclusion:** Achieves maximum efficiency (single loop, cached)
while maintaining clean, separated logic for the user.;

stop

@enduml